## Plan stage (domains 11-16)

Pure analysis, no runtime state. Takes WU declarations (Read/Write
AccessSets, scheduling hints, COMMUTATIVE flag) and produces a
complete execution plan:

1. Build DAG from AccessMask overlap (arvo-bitmask). DependencyGraph
   uses CSR backing (`row_offsets` + `col_indices` + `edge_kinds`)
   per Topic 9 axis B, bench-locked canonical at every N (no threshold).
   It converts to a bidirectional CSR (forward adjacency plus a
   pre-computed transpose) to feed the arvo-sparse structural steps
   (5-6). RAW edges are order-independent: for any unit pair where one
   writes a store the other reads, the writer sorts before the reader,
   regardless of which was registered first. WAW conflicts serialise
   deterministically by input index (the lower index first). WAR
   anti-dependencies are a tracked plan-chain follow-up.
2. Topological sort + node renumbering (arvo-graph). A registration
   whose units form a cyclic data dependency (each reads what another
   writes, transitively back to itself) cannot be linearised; the runner
   rejects it as `PlanError::Cycle`. A registration whose `PlanDims`
   declares more phases or trunks than the fixed-width `PhaseId` /
   `TrunkId` can name is rejected up front as
   `PlanError::PhaseCapacityExceedsIdWidth` /
   `PlanError::TrunkCapacityExceedsIdWidth`, so an over-wide dims fails
   loudly instead of silently wrapping ids past the addressable range.
3. Upward rank → critical path (arvo-graph)
4. Waist detection → phase boundaries (arvo-graph)
5. RCM reordering → renumber permutation on `ExecutionPlan`: row order is the WU execution order (feeds fiber grouping, step 8), column order is the arena layout (arvo-sparse)
6. Block diagonal → connected-component partition projected to per-phase
 trunk skeletons (one trunk per block; sets `Phase` id, `Trunk` id, trunk
 count). Dulmage-Mendelsohn fine decomposition, dead column elimination,
 and the phase-alignment check layer on in later rounds (arvo-sparse).
 Consumes `core_class: [CoreClass; MAX_CORES]` from heterogeneous-core
 detection per Topic 6 axis E.
7. Spectral partitioning → spectral `FiberGrouping` via an engine-local
 symmetric Laplacian over arvo-spectral's k-way partitioning, run over
 the live-node-count-aware (loose-CSR-correct) Laplacian. Step 8's
 width-gate consumes it for wide blocks (arvo-spectral).
8. Fiber grouping → per-block fiber formation projected onto
 `Trunk.components`. Each block (trunk) forms its fibers within the
 block's units in topo order, so fibers nest within trunks. A width-gate
 picks the former per block: wide blocks (unit count above the
 threshold) use the spectral former, narrow blocks the greedy one.
 `TrunkComponent::Fiber` entries carry id and units; columns, head+tail,
 and dispatch shape fill in at later steps (arvo-comb).
9. Per-fiber morsel sizing (write-only formula, domain 12). Output
   includes `micro_morsel_interval: USize` alongside `morsel_size: USize`
   per Topic 7 amendment. Default `MICRO_MORSEL_INTERVAL = 64` records.
   Adapt sidecar cache footprint subtracted from `l1_write_budget`
   per Topic 5 M10 amendment.
10. Per-phase adaptive configs (MAX_FUSE / BALANCED / MAX_SPLIT)
11. Column classification per fiber (internal / input / output)
12. Dirty propagation masks for incremental skip (arvo-bitmask)
13. FiberShape classification per Topic 3 amendment (Topic 4 axis D):
    typed projection over the unique fiber shape set (K, not 2^N).
    Codegen-emitted; informs per-core dispatch monomorphisation.

Plan recomputes on `Scheduler::run()` entry when the dirty bit is set
(Topic 1 axis 4). Dirty bit is keyed per `PlanAffecting`-bound Resource
(Topic 8 axis B); resources without that marker are cheap value-swaps.

Plan recompute is single-threaded in v1 (sequential through steps 1-13).
Parallelisation deferred until consumer pipelines push beyond ~256 WUs.

Incremental skip (domain 16) is distinct from that plan-recompute dirty bit.
Step 12 produces a per-unit predecessor mask keyed by carrier position (the
position the dispatch walk threads). At `Scheduler::run()` entry the engine
seeds a dirty set from per-root change signals: a `PlanAffecting` resource
swapped via `replace_resource`, a non-plan-affecting value swapped via
`replace_value`, an input the consumer marked changed, or the cold first frame
after build where every root is dirty. It propagates the seed forward over the
predecessor masks in carrier order (`dirty[j] |= dirty[i]` for every edge
`i to j`) and skips any unit whose entire predecessor set is clean: a unit whose
input cone did not change produces identical output whether it runs or not, so
skipping is correctness-preserving. This domain-16 dirty (which units to skip)
is separate from the domain-22 `PlanAffecting` dirty above (whether to rebuild
the plan); the latter's recompute-the-plan consumption is sequenced later with
the adapt subsystem.

The self-hosting meta-WU frame loop (`PlanStage`, `ScheduleReady`, `PassStart`,
`ScheduleEnd`) is the N-core self-hosting form. At one core the run path is the
static carrier walked over runtime record ranges plus this dirty gate, not a
meta-WU loop.

## Dispatch codegen (domain 17)

Turns the plan into executable code. Codegen is shaped by Topic 4 axes:

- Sealed `trait DispatchCodegen<Cfg: RunCfg>` with TAIT
  `type CoreDispatch = impl Fn(...)`. Single shipped impl
  `StandardCodegen` lowers to closed-form arithmetic, zero `blr`.
- Per-fiber-shape generic monomorphisation: `fn run_fiber<S: FiberShape>(...)`
  over the sealed `FiberShape: Sealed` marker carrying `type WuTuple` +
  `const SHAPE_ID`. Combinatorics K (unique shapes), not 2^N.
  Sealed-trait dispatch measured free vs hand-written direct calls
  per `mock/benches/dispatch_static_n*_findings.md`.
- Codegen-wrapped via `#[inline(always)] fn invoke_<wu>_in_fiber_<n>`
  shim fns; LLVM elides the shim layer.

The fused-pipeline pattern within a fiber:

1. Cache resources to stack-local array
2. Read fiber-input columns at morsel start
3. Pure function pipeline with locals (no intermediate stores)
4. Fiber-internal columns → register-to-register (DSE)
5. All output stores grouped at end (store-buffer friendly)

The concrete Context a WorkUnit's `execute` receives is a per-WU
`EngineCtx<'frame, R, W>` the engine constructs at the monomorphised
dispatch call site, scoped to that WU's declared `Read` / `Write`. It
holds only the projected pointers for those stores (a WU cannot reach
an undeclared store, since its Context does not carry that pointer),
implements the eight `HasX` accessor traits with `Provider = Self`,
resolves resources from the arena, reads and writes input columns over
the per-frame column buffers, appends to accumulator columns, and
iterates the morsel. The WorkUnit's
`Ctx<'frame>` stays a `HasX`-bounds declaration; only the engine names
`EngineCtx<'frame, W::Read, W::Write>`.

The sequential walk that constructs each unit's `EngineCtx` and runs
`execute` over a fiber's WorkUnit sequence is `RunFiber`, a monomorphic
trait over a value-carrying unit list: `WuCons` cells carry a unit
instance plus the tail, `WuNil` terminates, following the `PtrCons` /
`PtrNil` cell convention. Per unit it projects that unit's `EngineCtx`
from the bindings through `EngineCtx::project` (resources via `Project`,
read and write columns via `ColProject`, accumulators via `AccumProject`),
runs `execute`, and recurses on the tail; the per-unit projection indices
are carried as parallel witness lists and inferred at the call site, no
caller turbofish. No unit dispatches through a stored function pointer, so
a fiber's unit sequence monomorphises into one straight-line body that
devirtualizes under fat LTO. A column-reading or column-writing unit
dispatches through this walk directly, projecting its columns alongside its
resources; there is no separate erased-slot dispatch path. This is the
single-core dispatch core, pure Rust generics, distinct from the
per-fiber-shape `run_fiber<S: FiberShape>` monomorphisation above (the
codegen-emitted parallel-path shape). The walk is driven from
`Scheduler::run`, where the bindings type is fixed by the scheduler so the
column projection's associated-type ties resolve.

Directly above `RunFiber` in the canonical hierarchy
(`pipeline -> core -> phase ↔ waist -> trunk -> fiber`) is the trunk: a
trunk is a sequence of fibers. The value-carrying trunk carrier is
`FiberCons<F, Rest>` / `FiberNil`, a cons-list of fibers mirroring the
`WuCons` / `WuNil` unit carrier, where each `FiberCons` cell holds one fiber
(itself a `WuCons` unit list) plus the tail of remaining fibers. It lives in
`hilavitkutin-api::work_unit_values` next to `WuCons` so the scheduler builder
can construct it. `RunTrunk<A, WL>` is the monomorphic walk over that carrier,
the level above `RunFiber`: `WL` is a cons-list whose head is one fiber's
witness list and whose tail is the rest, so the trunk-over-fiber nest is a
2-deep witness cons-list inferred at the call site with no turbofish. The base
impl on `FiberNil` is a no-op; the `FiberCons<F, Rest>` impl bounds
`F: RunFiber<A, FW>` and `Rest: RunTrunk<A, RestWL>`, running each fiber
through the unchanged `RunFiber::run` and recursing on the rest. On a single
core the fibers run sequentially, the same order a flat concatenation of their
unit lists would produce, so a trunk walk is output-equivalent to a flat fiber
walk over the concatenated units. The delegation adds no indirect call, so
`RunTrunk` devirtualises under fat LTO exactly as `RunFiber` does. At many
cores each trunk, not each fiber, becomes the core-pinned unit and a phase's
trunks run concurrently with zero cross-trunk synchronisation (their write
columns are disjoint); that core-pinning is a following round.

Two levels sit above the trunk. A phase is a set of trunks; the value-carrying
phase carrier is `TrunkCons<T, Rest>` / `TrunkNil`, a cons-list of trunks
mirroring `FiberCons` / `FiberNil`, each cell holding one trunk (itself a
`FiberCons` fiber list) plus the tail. `RunPhase<A, WL>` walks it, delegating
each trunk to `RunTrunk` and recursing. The pipeline is the sequence of phases;
its carrier is `PhaseCons<P, Rest>` / `PhaseNil`, and `RunPipeline<A, WL>` walks
it, running each phase through `RunPhase` and then arriving at a waist barrier
before the next phase. The waist is the phase boundary, the point every trunk of
a phase reaches before the next phase begins. At a single core the barrier is a
degenerate one-arriver (an `AtomicUsize` counter that never spins when the
expected arriver count is one), present so the waist composes into the nest
without breaking devirt; the load-bearing N-core waist is the shipped
`phase_barrier_arrive`, wired in a following round. The full
`RunPipeline -> RunPhase -> RunTrunk -> RunFiber` nest is a 3-deep witness
cons-list inferred at the call site with no turbofish, and the whole nest plus
the waist barrier folds into one straight-line body that devirtualises under fat
LTO. On a single core every level runs its children sequentially and the waist
is a no-op, so a pipeline walk is output-equivalent to the flat `RunFiber` walk
over the units concatenated in the same order. That nested carrier is one
exploration of the trunk and phase levels; the canonical single-core mechanism
is the const-gated per-trunk dispatch below, which partitions over the flat
carrier rather than building a nested carrier type.

The single-core path `Scheduler::run` drives is a const-gated per-trunk dispatch
over the flat `WuCons` carrier. The carrier stays flat; the phase and trunk
partition lives entirely in the compile-time grouping (`plan::grouping`, which
folds each unit's read and write access sets into masks and derives, as const
functions, the waist-bounded phase and the within-phase column-conflict trunk of
every carrier position). `RunGatedTrunk` is the per-trunk walk: it threads the
carrier position as a Peano witness and gates each cell on whether that position
belongs to the trunk being run, so monomorphising it for one trunk lets
dead-code elimination collapse the walk to that trunk's members only, a
devirt-clean member-only program. An outer driver walks the carrier once per
trunk-root, dispatching each trunk-root's per-trunk mono in phase order, with a
degenerate single-core waist between phases. A trunk lies wholly within one
phase, so a trunk is identified by its trunk id alone; phase order is enforced
by the driver's phase loop, not by a second gate. Because each trunk runs its
members in carrier (RCM-reordered topological) order and the phases run in
dependency order, the result is output-equivalent to the flat `RunFiber` walk,
while every trunk becomes an independently monomorphised program ready for the
core-pinning of a following round.

`Scheduler::run` dispatches the WorkUnit instances the builder retained at
registration (see the scheduler builder section), windowing the record range
into morsels of `RunCfg::MORSEL_SIZE` (guarded to at least one record). It
walks the plan's `phases -> trunks -> fibers` structure. For single-core
execution it drives the const-gated per-trunk dispatch above: each trunk's
member-only mono in turn, in phase order, over the units `group_fibers` placed in
RCM-reordered topological order, projecting each unit's columns and resources
inline through the same `RunFiber` per-unit step. The parallel `run_parallel`
path drives the same const-gated per-trunk monos through a core-aware walk: each
worker walks the carrier and, at each in-phase trunk-root, fires that trunk's
`RunGatedTrunk` mono only when the within-phase trunk rank modulo core count
equals its core (`rank % ncores == core`), so a core runs exactly the trunks it
owns and a non-owned trunk's body is dead-code-eliminated from the core's
program. The head+tail convergence branch (a single-trunk waist-bounded phase
split by record range across cores) keeps its record-slice ownership. Either way
there is no type-erased per-unit dispatch slot: the column-capable walk is the
only dispatch path.

Single-core dispatch runs each fiber's units in the plan's RCM-reordered
topological order. RCM (step 5 of the plan chain) produces two orderings from
the access matrix: a row reordering that is the WU execution order, and a
column reordering that is the arena memory layout. The row reordering stays
topological (every unit runs after its dependencies), but among units at the
same topological depth, which a naive topological sort would order
arbitrarily, RCM picks the order that maximises column reuse between adjacent
units. `group_fibers` (step 8) processes units in this RCM-reordered topo
order, so each fiber's unit sequence is already in it. The dispatch walk runs
that sequence as a compile-time-known local cons-list, which is what
devirtualizes: a monomorphised body over a local sequence of known unit
identities inlines under fat LTO, whereas a runtime-indexed call through a
stored order array does not (the indirect-dispatch anti-pattern). The column
reordering sets `rcm_order` for arena layout, co-locating co-accessed columns
so a morsel's reads land in adjacent cache lines.

The phase and trunk grouping is parallel-scheduling structure: a phase is a
cross-core sync barrier, a trunk is a core assignment. On a single core the
barrier and the core assignment collapse to program order, but the phase
sequence and the within-fiber RCM order are preserved, because both encode
dependency and cache-locality structure that holds on one core too. Realizing
the RCM order as the compile-time order of the dispatch body is the codegen
flattener's job (the per-core compiled dispatch below): the per-fiber, or
single-core whole-schedule, monomorphised body is emitted with its units in
the plan's RCM-reordered order. A unit whose dependencies admit no topological
order is a build-time `BuildError`, the right place for a malformed dependency
graph.

The nesting is per-fiber, chosen by the fiber's `morsel_local` bit. An
accumulator-free fiber dispatches morsel-outer: one morsel runs the fiber's
whole unit sequence before the next, so its intermediate columns stay
cache-resident across the units. This is sound because the per-record `read`
/ `write` accessors are morsel-relative and the only cross-record surface is
the accumulator append path, so an accumulator-free fiber has every cross-unit
dependency morsel-local. An accumulator-bearing fiber stays unit-outer (a unit
completes its record range before the next runs), the cross-record-safe form.
An accumulator-bearing pipeline still runs its accumulator-free fibers
morsel-outer while its accumulator fibers stay unit-outer.

A fiber-internal column, written and read entirely inside one fiber per the
`classify_columns` Input / Output / Internal split, is backed by morsel-sized
stack scratch rather than an arena column, so dead-store elimination collapses
the intermediate traffic under fat LTO and only a fiber's true output columns
reach arena memory. This within-fiber fusion plus the devirtualized walk are
the two mechanisms that bring single-core dispatch to parity with a hand-fused
loop. Windowing the record range into multiple morsels with micro-morsel sync
points (the morsel-loop split) is a later round; `run` covers all records in
one morsel.

The emitted single-core dispatch monos are guarded by a standing ASM checklist,
the mechanical complement to the engine-vs-std perf oracle. Where the perf oracle
measures the outcome (throughput against an optimal hand-written loop), the ASM
checklist measures the mechanism directly: it disassembles each representative
dispatch shape (the column-chain `run`, the fused `run_fused`, the accumulator
carrier, the dirty-gated frame) and asserts the absence of an indirect call in
the inner body as a hard invariant, plus indexed column addressing, a stack-free
inner loop, and a baked immediate morsel where the plan fixes it as a quality
report. A surviving indirect call means dispatch is no longer devirtualized, and
the checklist names the regressing symbol regardless of whether the slowdown
surfaced in a particular bench size.

The realized within-fiber fusion for a linear chain is a type-level fold. A
fiber whose units are each a pure per-record map (the opt-in fusion contract in
the api crate: read one column, write one column, value to value) and which
link as a read-after-write chain folds into a single fused unit whose body runs
the maps as composed monomorphised calls, every intermediate a register local,
only the chain's final output column stored. The fold is the canonical spec's
deep-single-fiber rust-pipe: it matches a hand-fused loop (3 loads / 3 stores
for a four-stage chain, the internal columns eliminated, zero indirect calls),
proven by the fusion sketches. The scheduler exposes a fused dispatch entry that
folds the registered carrier and walks the fused unit through the same
`RunFiber` path; the per-WU walk stays the entry for non-fusible carriers
(accumulators). The dispatch choice is an explicit entry, not a transparent
auto-detection: a default-plus-specializing dispatch is not expressible on the
toolchain (the fused projection witness is an unconstrained specializing-impl
parameter, and `min_specialization` does not permit specializing on the
fusion-contract bound), so a fusible fiber opts in by calling the fused entry
while the engine still performs the fold. This linear fold is the deep
single-fiber case; a multi-fiber DAG reaches parity through the per-fiber
compiled dispatch above, not through linear folding.

Per-core compiled dispatch: each physical core gets a monomorphised
`CoreProgram<...>` encoding its phases, record ranges, morsel
boundaries, and sync points. Worker entry receives
`Pin<&'frame CoreProgram<...>>` per the Topic 6 axis C lifetime
contract (validated by the PoolFrame lifetime sketch).

Progress counters: `AtomicUsize` per fiber, monotonic record index.
Plain store (Release), not RMW; cheapest atomic per the
`atomic_ordering_cost` bench (Relaxed beats Acquire/SeqCst by 3-4x
on aarch64). Lock-free by construction.

`DispatchApproach` shipped in v1 (`#[non_exhaustive]`):
- `ScheduleMega`: approach A.
- `TrunkMega`: approach B (within margin of A per bench).
- `IndirectPerFiber`: approach D + E. Single-target indirect dispatch
  per fiber; BTB-warm above N≈256 per the `dispatch_dynamic` bench
  (volatile fn pointer converges to baseline once warmed).

Selection driven by record count + per-fiber shape entropy. Codegen
emits `dmb st` (aarch64) / `sfence` (x86_64) between final FiberOutput
non-temporal `stnp` writes and progress-counter Release store
(Topic 3 invariant S3).

ExpandedLto pragma (domain 03, hilavitkutin-build) REQUIRED for
devirtualisation.

## select_approach base heuristic

`select_approach(record_count, fiber_count) -> DispatchApproach`
returns one of `IndirectPerFiber`, `TrunkMega`, `ScheduleMega` per
the three-variant codegen contract. Base body ships a three-branch
heuristic over `(record_count, fiber_count)` using typed `USize`
thresholds. The two cutovers live as named constants:
`SCHEDULE_MEGA_THRESHOLD: USize = USize(10_000)` and
`SINGLE_FIBER_CUTOVER: USize = USize(1)`. The function body
compares `record_count` and `fiber_count` against the typed
constants directly (`PartialOrd` on `USize`), so the bare-primitive
literal lives at one declaration site instead of every call site.

- `record_count >= SCHEDULE_MEGA_THRESHOLD` returns `ScheduleMega`.
  At high record counts LLVM's whole-pipeline optimisation window
  outweighs the indirect-call overhead.
- Otherwise, if `fiber_count <= SINGLE_FIBER_CUTOVER` returns
  `TrunkMega`. Single fiber means the mega function fits the
  working set without the per-fiber dispatch split.
- Otherwise returns `IndirectPerFiber`. Per-fiber indirect call is
  the lowest-overhead split at small record counts with many
  fibers.

The `10_000` threshold matches the `<10K target` named in the type
doc comments on `DispatchApproach` and `select_approach`. The
tiebreaker between `TrunkMega` and `IndirectPerFiber` uses a coarse
single-fiber cut. Benchmark-tuned thresholds (both the size cutover
AND the fiber-count cutover) land in the follow-up; see BACKLOG.

## codegen_fiber + codegen_core skeleton stubs

`codegen_fiber<Ctx, MAX_CORES>() -> FiberDispatch<Ctx, MAX_CORES>`
and `codegen_core<Ctx, MAX_FIBERS>() -> CoreDispatch<Ctx, MAX_FIBERS>`
ship as skeleton-return stubs in the runtime megaround follow-up.
Both delegate to the sibling constructor: `codegen_fiber` returns
`FiberDispatch::new()` (empty record with `body: Maybe::Isnt`,
zero-init metadata); `codegen_core` returns `CoreDispatch::new()`
(array of empty `FiberDispatch` records plus zero-init phases and
boundaries).

The skeleton shape exists today because the runtime megaround
shipped both sibling constructors. This step promotes the
constructors from "exists, unused" to "exists, called by the
codegen entry points", which removes the last two `todo!()` calls
from `dispatch/mod.rs`. `Scheduler::run` remains the only engine
surface that panics after this lands.

